// Verifying an Ed25519 signature — six refusals, one `bool`, and the one place in cryptography where
// that is not merely lossy.
//
// Found by sweeping the tree for `bool` functions with three or more `return false` sites. **78 of
// them, collapsing 405 refusals**; of those, **17 are verifications collapsing 106**, across `tls`,
// `crypto`, `bls`, `tor`, `ssh`, `webrtc`, `ssz` and `lightclient`. Two were already rewritten here —
// `@/packages/lightclient`'s `validateUpdate` and `@/packages/ssz`'s branch check — and this is the
// smallest of the seventeen, which is why it is the clearest.

import { Result, Bytes } from "core";
import { Digest32 } from "./digest.wac";

/**
 * **Whether it verified — which is an answer, not a fault.**
 *
 * Added 2026-09-05, and it is the third instance of *normal but uninteresting is not a fault* after
 * `@/packages/lightclient`'s `NotRelevant` and `@/packages/datetime`'s `LeapSecond`. It is the first
 * where conflating the two means something.
 *
 * `DoesNotVerify` was a member of `VerifyFault`, so the answer to *does this signature check out*
 * arrived through `Err` — and a caller writing `try verify(…)` propagates **signature invalid** as
 * an error indistinguishable from **this is not a signature**. For a consensus client those want
 * opposite responses: an invalid signature is a message to drop, and a malformed key is a peer to
 * stop trusting.
 *
 * Exported from `crypto` because `@/packages/bls` answers the same question and had the same member.
 * §1.
 */
export enum Verified { Valid, Invalid }

/** The key was not 32 bytes. A caller's bug, not an input's. */
export struct BadKeyLength { i32 got; }
/** The signature was not 64 bytes. */
export struct BadSignatureLength { i32 got; }
/**
 * `S` is at or above the group order `L`.
 *
 * RFC 8032 §5.1.7 requires refusing this, and the shipped comment says exactly why: *"what stops a
 * signature being malleable: without it, S and S+L both verify and a signature is no longer a unique
 * token."*
 *
 * **This is the refusal that is a policy**, and see below.
 */
export struct NonCanonicalScalar { }
/** The public key does not decode to a point on the curve. */
export struct BadPublicKey { }
/** `R` does not decode to a point on the curve. */
export struct BadR { }
/*
 * `DoesNotVerify` was here, described as *"the honest answer, and the only one of the six that is
 * about the signature"* — which is exactly the argument for it not being in this union. It is
 * `Verified.Invalid` now. **The file said why and then put it in anyway.**
 */
export union<BadKeyLength, BadSignatureLength, NonCanonicalScalar, BadPublicKey, BadR>
  VerifyFault;

/**
 * `[S]B == R + [k]A`, or which of the five input rules refused.
 *
 * The shipped `ed25519Verify` has all six as `return false` and the arithmetic is identical; the
 * changes are that a caller can tell them apart, and that **the sixth is not one of them** — it is
 * the `Ok`, carrying `Valid` or `Invalid`.
 */
export Result<Verified, VerifyFault> verify(Bytes pub, Bytes msg, Bytes sig) { … }

// ## 1. Three kinds of refusal wearing one answer
//
// The six are not one thing:
//
//   * **A caller's bug** — `BadKeyLength`. A 31-byte public key came from this program, not from a
//     peer. `@/packages/lightclient/src/branch.wac` found the same split and called it *the same
//     fault set is a caller's bug in one consumer and untrusted input in another*; here the two are
//     in **one function**, which is a stronger version of it.
//   * **Malformed input** — `BadSignatureLength`, `BadPublicKey`, `BadR`. A peer sent something that
//     is not a signature. A system scoring peers wants these.
//   * **The answer** — `DoesNotVerify`. The signature is well-formed and wrong.
//
// A caller logging *"signature invalid"* on `BadKeyLength` is debugging the wrong program, and one
// that drops a peer on `DoesNotVerify` and on `BadR` alike cannot tell a corrupt link from a hostile
// one.
//
// ## 2. And `NonCanonicalScalar` is a **policy**, which is why this matters more here than elsewhere
//
// The other five are facts. That one is a rule the verifier chose to apply, and Ed25519 is the
// well-known case where implementations differ about exactly this class of check — canonicality of
// `S`, canonical point encodings, small-order points, and whether the equation is checked with the
// cofactor. Two libraries can both answer `false` for the same signature and disagree about *which
// rule* refused it, and a third can answer `true`.
//
// **A `bool` makes that disagreement invisible.** Where it costs is a system with more than one
// implementation deciding the same question — a consensus, a federation, a client and a server that
// must agree on which messages exist. A signature one side accepts and the other refuses is a
// partition, and the shape that produces it is exactly *the strict check is optional and the answer
// does not say whether it ran*.
//
// Naming the refusal does not make two implementations agree. It makes the disagreement **findable**:
// a differential over a corpus can report *A said `NonCanonicalScalar` and B said `Ok`* rather than
// *A said no and B said yes*, and the first is a bug report while the second is a mystery.
//
// This directory's entry on a fault union ending *"where the next thing down cannot fail in more
// than one way"* has its counterexample here: below `verify` is field arithmetic, which cannot
// refuse — so by that rule six members is the end of the chain, and it is also the beginning of the
// only chain that matters.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **Nothing says which of the six are optional.** `NonCanonicalScalar` is required by RFC 8032 and
// omitted by real implementations; a `union` lists members and cannot mark one *this verifier's
// strictness rather than the format's*. Two unions would say it —
// `union<Malformed, Strictness, DoesNotVerify>` — and that is the nesting the flattening entry is
// about, used to encode **provenance of a rule** rather than a family of faults. Third use of nesting
// found in this directory and the first where the grouping is about who decided the rule.
//
// **The lengths are checked and the types could have.** `pub` is a `Bytes` and must be 32; `sig` must
// be 64. `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has the six packages that want a length in a type, and this is a
// seventh — and the first where the missing bound produces a *fault member* rather than a guard, so
// the cost is visible in the public surface rather than in a body.
//
// **`msg` is unbounded and that is correct and unstated.** Ed25519 hashes the whole message, so
// there is no length limit and no streaming variant here; `sha512` over `64 + msg.len()` bytes is
// built as one array. A caller verifying a large message allocates it twice. `@/packages/ssz` wanted
// a streaming digest for the same reason and got one; this file's `kInput` is the shape that made it
// worth wanting.
